  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{x}^{2}+3x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{x}^{2}+3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{2x}-1]^{\u2032}}{[{x}^{2}+3x]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2(0)}}{2(0)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
